{smcl}
{title:ccctrends — Untreated trend diagnostics}
{pstd}{cmd:ccctrends} outcome covariates [if] [in]{cmd:, group(}region{cmd:) time(}period{cmd:) gvar(}cohort{cmd:)} [{cmd:panels(core|all)} {cmd:controls(never)} {cmd:reference(zero|pooled|matrixname)} {cmd:matchmatrix(name)} {cmd:name(graphname)} {cmd:saving(file.dta)} {cmd:csv(file.csv)} {cmd:export(file.png)} {cmd:replace nograph}]
{pstd}Standalone; no preceding CCC test required. All displayed dates precede the earliest focal adoption. Restrict focal groups or the window using if/in; retain intended controls. Fixed never-treated controls and complete cell support are required. Not-yet-treated plot controls are not implemented. Test and plot samples differ.
{pstd}Core displays unconditional means, homogeneous covariate adjustment, and integrated changes. All adds region-, time-, and cell-varying adjustments. Individual panel names: raw, homogeneous, region, time, twoway, integrated. All models retain cell intercepts. Adjustments subtract (z-z_ref)'beta, never full fitted values. Zero is an explicit partial-residual convention; pooled uses the observation-pooled plot basis mean. An explicit reference is a 1 by K Stata matrix.
{pstd}Integrated changes use unrestricted cell fits and the treated target-period mean basis in all four predictions, including baseline/control endpoints. Adjacent observed periods can be unequal intervals; r(series) records the baseline. They are changes, not outcome levels or necessarily annual changes. Means of the actual basis (including powers and indicators) are used. No confidence bands.
{pstd}Default weights are equal across focal regions and equal across controls within each focal region. {cmd:matchmatrix()} has four columns: focal code, control code, focal weight, control weight. Codes are sorted policy-region indices among the plot sample, as reported by {cmd:r(group_1)}, etc. Every focal must have normalized control weights; focal weights sum to one. Self-matches and duplicate pairs are rejected. Weights are descriptive region/comparison weights, not survey weights.
{title:Stored results}
{pstd}{cmd:r(series)} columns panel, time, baseline, treated, control, gap. Panels 1–6 correspond to raw/H/R/T/TW/integrated. {cmd:r(pairs)} records codes, endpoints, weights, four predictions, two changes, target means, four endpoint intercept/slope vectors and four range flags. An outside flag indicates some target basis value exceeds that endpoint's marginal ranges; this is not a joint-support guarantee. {cmd:r(coefficients)}: model (1–4), region, period, intercept, K slopes. {cmd:r(cells)}: region, period, cell N, mean outcome, K basis means. {cmd:r(reference)}, {cmd:r(matches)}, N, K and basis are also returned. Saving/CSV exports the aggregate series; the other matrices can be exported with svmat in a preserved dataset.
{pstd}Graphs are native editable Stata graphs. Use graph save for .gph or export PNG/PDF/SVG. replace is explicit for output files and named combined graphs. No input data, prior estimation result, RNG state or working directory is changed. Requested unidentified panels fail visibly. The current implementation fits all models even for an individual panel.
{title:Interpretation}
{pstd}These are diagnostics, not tests establishing parallel trends. Common-slope adjustment is not a complete pointwise CPT test. Changing reference covariates can change heterogeneous adjusted shapes. Integrated Parallel Trends is the chapter's current terminology; intersection is legacy terminology. Equal weights and reference normalization are software conventions; this is not a reproduction of the empirical figures.
